  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n   - We will approach the problem using a multi-phase optimization strategy inspired by the original program, but with key improvements to achieve a better objective.\n   - The central idea is to use independent coordinate updates (ICU) with momentum, but combined with a novel temperature-based peak pruning method to focus optimization on the most critical components.\n   - Unlike the original which used exponential weights for updates, our approach uses a momentum-ICC (Independent Coordinate Conservation) technique that better preserves the \"shape\" of promising solutions while exploring.\n   - We also enhance the search by considering randomized interpolation factors, which allows introducing diversity even in the finest optimization stage.\n   - The improvement lies in better handling of peak misalignment in the convolution and more aggressive exploration strategies.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n   - We work with normalized sequences (sum=1) to leverage scale-invariance of the objective function.\n   - The objective function is O = 2*n * max(b) / (sum(a))^2, where b is the convolution with mode 'full'.\n   - We define a momentum-based gradient update that penalizes ad-hoc peak repair, steering the optimization towards stable, energy-conserving \"wave forms\" rather than random spikes.\n   - Peak pruning is introduced using a temperature parameter to dynamically ignore outlier peaks in iterative optimization, effectively focusing computational resources.\n   - To recover from poor updates, we enforce that any coordinate update must preserve the maximum contribution within its proximity.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   a. Initialization:\n        1. If parent (height_sequence_1) is available, use its normalized form (ensure sum=1) as base candidate.\n        2. If no parent, start from a robust interpolated initial guess (640-length sequence) normalized to sum=1.\n        3. For each candidate, compute its objective (evaluate_sequence).\n\n   b. Core optimization loop:\n        1. Normalize the sequence to sum=1.\n        2. Set up momentum vector for gradient descent.\n        3. Project to simplex immediately after any negative update.\n        4. Compute convolution b = convolve(a, a) via mode 'full'.\n        5. Identify the top peaks (k candidates) in b, but prune them using a temperature-dependent threshold to avoid overwhelming the optimizer with every fluctuation.\n        6. Update only the coordinates contributing to the top peaks, but repair any damage gracefully.\n\n   c. Specific updates:\n        1. Momentum update: f_nest = f * exp(momentum * v), but adapted to our enhanced peak pruning.\n        2. We use a windowed iterative update approach, focusing on only 50% of the original problem's coordinates when stable.\n\n   d. Search phases:\n        Phase I: Coarse regularization (500 steps) \n        Phase II: Peak-prune refinement (100 steps) \n        Phase III: Local multimodal polishing \n\n   e. Candidate acceptance:\n        Any solution from an optimization phase that shows a meaningful improvement in objective value is compared with the current best, accepting only those that meet stricter validity criteria.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n   - Initialization: Strong structured initialization, matching parent scale if available, normalized.\n   -Peak pruning: This is our unique contribution; using temperature and entropy to focus the search.\n   - Multi-peak search: We will search in up to 3 independent neighborhoods (sharp peaks, steep valleys, plateau) for the optimal shape.\n   - Step counting variation: We use the concept of \"activation counts\" to avoid degenerate solutions.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n   - We allocate 1500 seconds for peak-prune optimization and 300 seconds for local polishing.\n   - We protect against: \n        *np.nan/np.inf updates*\n        *Conversion failure from parent*\n        *Inconsistent peak updates that destroy the current best*\n\n6. Explicit validation and return contract\n   - Upon completion, we reevaluate the final candidate with multiple passes to ensure consistency.\n   - Only if strictly better than the initial parent (if any) or uniform fallback, we return the optimized solution.",
